---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Base title="Surf">
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="brand-mark">Surf</p>
      <h1>Remote browser on hardware you already have</h1>
      <p class="deck">
        Surf runs Chromium on a Windows, macOS, or Linux computer and streams
        that page to a native app. The app draws the chrome (tabs, address bar,
        library) and sends input back. Page content never goes through the
        device's own web engine.
      </p>
      <ul class="cta-row">
        <li><a class="cta cta-primary" href={`${base}install/host/`}>Install the host</a></li>
        <li><a class="cta cta-secondary" href={`${base}install/ios/`}>iOS client</a></li>
        <li><a class="cta cta-secondary" href={`${base}install/desktop/`}>Linux client</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        The host owns a Chromium profile: cookies, JavaScript, downloads, and
        site sign-in all happen there. The client opens a pinned TLS connection,
        shows the live stream, and forwards touch, keyboard, pointer, and files.
        You pair once with a QR code or a short code, then confirm the six words
        match on both ends.
      </p>
      <p>
        Surf is not a hosted service. There is no account and no shared password.
        Run the host on your LAN, or on any address the client can reach. Each
        device keeps its own key. Clients pin the host certificate and refuse a
        different fingerprint.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>In practice</h2>
      <div class="point">
        <p class="point-title">Chromium does the browsing</p>
        <p>
          You get whatever that desktop Chromium can load. For awkward logins,
          open Browser setup on the computer, finish there, then continue from
          the client.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="point">
        <p class="point-title">The client is a browser UI</p>
        <p>
          Tabs, suggestions, bookmarks, history, downloads, find, and reader
          are built into the app. It is not a full-desktop remote-desktop
          session.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="point">
        <p class="point-title">Data stays on the host</p>
        <p>
          Identity, pairings, and the browser profile live under
          <code>SURF_HOME</code>. Revoke a device from the host when you want
          it gone.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Clients</h2>
      <div class="point">
        <p class="point-title">
          <a href={`${base}install/ios/`}>iOS / iPadOS 6-14</a>
        </p>
        <p>
          Jailbreak package for iOS 6 through 14. Install steps (Cydia / Sileo)
          are on the iOS page.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="point">
        <p class="point-title">
          <a href={`${base}install/desktop/`}>Linux</a>
        </p>
        <p>
          Desktop client for the same host and pairing flow.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Docs</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={`${base}install/host/`}>Host install</a>,
        <a href={`${base}docs/guide/pairing/`}>pairing</a>,
        <a href={`${base}docs/guide/overview/`}>overview</a>,
        <a href={`${base}docs/reference/security/`}>security</a>,
        <a href={`${base}docs/`}>full index</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
